# Raw pressure suite 01: genuine uncovered cancellation gate, with source cause

The overall suite remains **FAIL**. Phase 1 (pressure) passed; phase 2 (partial cancellation) was
admitted and failed because the required two-peer pending-P8 boundary never occurred. No abort was
issued, no cancellation/ACK/reuse result exists, and phase 3 was not admitted. The failure is not a
model exception, corrupt observer sequence, or an auditor false negative. It exposes insufficient
headroom in the original cancellation workload under the pinned Ascend allocation predictor.

Run: `runs/raw-pressure-suite-hccl-true-01`. All original files remain unchanged. Controller exit 1,
shutdown returned, owned processes absent, and four physical NPUs empty. The full observer trace
SHA-256 is `ce24363d20b230703238efcbe148a8d1794eb9277507747c2dbdbbc0ade58895`.

## What actually happened

Phase 1 completed all three original outputs in 129 steps, observed 64 allocation-None events,
and observed the seed D being preempted while the pending P retained its 15 physical blocks. Its
post-phase boundary returned all 19 usable blocks with no request, held ownership, cohort or ACK.
The provisional pre-shared-shutdown audit is false only because shutdown had intentionally not yet
occurred; its final phase audit is true.

Phase 2 began with that clean 19-block baseline. Derived trace sequence numbers below retain the
original global sequence in `phase-2-compact-trace.json` (global sequence = derived + 906):

| Sequence | Observation |
| --- | --- |
| 3 | Seed P128 allocated four blocks: attention 1 plus three Mamba groups 1 each. |
| 9 | First seed D allocation succeeded without allocating any new physical block. |
| 11, 13 | Each peer P4080 allocated seven blocks; both peers admitted; one free block remained. |
| 16 | Both peers pending at P4; seed computed 129 tokens and had two sampled outputs. |
| 18–19 | Next seed D (one token) allocation returned None with one free block; held groups remained `[1,1,1,1]`. |
| 20–21 | Seed preempted, releasing its four blocks; peer ownership stayed intact. |
| 22–24 | No decode was scheduled. The pending peers advanced from P4 directly to P40 and each produced its first token. |
| 32–50 | Seed recomputation later formed a different cohort. Its P8 is not the required two-peer boundary. The bounded driver stopped when that cohort reached P12. |

The exact driver guard is `pressure_phase.py:99–116`. It correctly requires both original peer IDs
at P8 and does not accept an unrelated cohort's progress. The observer preserved 51 contiguous,
error-free rows for this phase. Both requests really left pending P before an abort was attempted.

## Source explanation and CPU reproduction

Pinned Core `d0f22d2b...`, Ascend `03766ac...`, plugin `f7ed21e...` were not modified.
`source-identities.json` hashes the audited files. The extra three blocks are an **allocation
estimate**, not evidence of actual temporary Mamba allocation or copy-on-write.

1. Core `kv_cache_manager.py:366–413` supplies `total_computed_tokens=129` and
   `num_tokens_main_model=130` for a normal running decode. No external KV is supplied.
2. Core `single_type_kv_cache_manager.py:102–186` needs zero new blocks for the held Mamba page.
   The `none` Mamba path delegates to this implementation at lines 1206–1219.
3. Ascend `patch_mamba_manager.py:67–114`, in its 0.25.1 compatibility branch, computes
   `local_hit_tokens = len(new_computed_blocks) * block_size = 0`. It then treats
   `129 > 0` as external tokens and `130 > 129` as synchronous new work, adding one block **after**
   the Core result. It does so even for an already-running request. Three Mamba managers yield an
   estimated need of three blocks, so Core's `required_blocks > available_blocks` gate rejects
   `3 > 1` before allocation. The native coordinator calls with its older five-positional-argument
   interface; successful calls also imply this compatibility branch (the other branch asserts a
   missing `num_tokens_main_model`). The historical controller did not record `VLLM_VERSION`
   explicitly; branch attribution is supported by this call signature, not a new runtime hook.
4. Plugin `decoupled_scheduler.py:368–376` finishes a pending prefill when no decode survives.
   `decoupled_worker.py:335–339` removes the layer quota for that instruction. This accounts for
   the observed P4→P40 receipt and the unavailable P8 boundary.

`replay_allocation_predictor.py` contains exact AST-extracted predictor method bodies and synthetic
objects populated from observed occupancy. It imports no vLLM, Torch, model, or device module.
`predictor-results.json` records five assertions: native zero vs Ascend one for the ordinary
running request (with and without a cached-block marker), equal fresh-prefill estimates, and the
separate new-external synchronous/asynchronous adjustment branches. The external cases show the
condition's intended separate input category; they do not certify the whole external-KV allocator
or claim that its extra page is physically allocated in this model configuration.

An independent source review by the performance agent agreed with the cause. The original
planner's physical occupancy arithmetic `4 + 7 + 7 = 18` was accurate, but failed to account for
this conservative Ascend predictor. It was insufficient to guarantee P8 coverage.

## Smallest bounded follow-up

Keep native/plugin sources and every old result frozen. The new explicit cancellation treatment
uses **22 total blocks**: one null +18 held +three free. At seed129→130 the exact predictor need3
then meets free3. Request prompts/output budgets, seed-first admission, two-peer P8 abort,
partial/full selection, ACK ownership, physical reuse, 870-step/1800-second bounds, and original
execute/audit functions remain unchanged. The four reuse pairs peak at14 held blocks, leaving7
free. Peer outputs remain within4096 and seed within224, so this workload does not cross another
attention allocation boundary. These are CPU feasibility findings; actual P8/ACK/reuse must still
pass on hardware.

The old planner greedily chooses P4096 with pool22, so merely invoking it again would change the
workload. `../pressure_headroom.py` instead validates the original pool20 plan and makes a narrowly
specified pool22 derivation while delegating to the unchanged original execute/audit. New plans,
CPU validation and commands are in `../cancellation-headroom-v1/`. They are separate experiments,
not repairs to the old failed result. The original pool20 pressure-only plan remains a meaningful
allocation/preemption test; it need not be inflated.

An isolated native fix may later preserve the already-running request fast path before the
external-KV adjustment, with true external-KV and cache-hit regression coverage. No native fix is
included in this campaign, and all quality/performance source identities remain unchanged.
